 SRE Interview Reality Check (Read Before Your Next Interview) 🚨


“These questions keep coming in SRE / DevOps interviews…
What should I answer in real production terms?”

So I’m sharing this for every fresher, mid-level, and experienced engineer preparing for SRE roles 👇

⸻

🔹 Do companies always use Amazon Linux in production?
No. OS choice depends on project, security, and application compatibility. Amazon Linux is common, but Ubuntu is equally used in many real environments.

🔹 Is AWS usage in production just console login?
Absolutely not. Production access is controlled using IAM roles, MFA, SSO, CLI, and CI/CD automation. Manual console work is minimal.

🔹 Team structure in real SRE teams?
SREs work closely with developers, QA, DB, network, and security teams. Reliability is a shared responsibility.

🔹 Critical issues handled in production?
High CPU, memory leaks, pod crashes, disk full, failed deployments, misconfigured load balancers — real problems, real pressure.

🔹 On-call & incident handling?
Late-night alerts, incident bridges with 5+ teams, client updates, RCA calls, and post-mortems. This is where engineers are truly tested.

🔹 Kubernetes clusters & nodes?
Multiple clusters per environment. Nodes scale based on load using autoscaling, not fixed numbers.

🔹 Applications in infrastructure?
Usually 10–30+ microservices, isolated via namespaces, monitored and deployed independently.

🔹 Day-to-day SRE work?
Monitoring, incident response, CI/CD, Terraform, Kubernetes ops, automation, and reliability improvements — not just deployments.

🔹 EC2 instances in AWS?
Instances serve different roles: K8s nodes, monitoring, CI/CD, bastion, apps — and they scale dynamically.

⸻

🎯 Interview truth most candidates miss:

Interviewers don’t want perfect numbers.
They want production thinking, clarity, and calm problem-solving.

If you’re answering interviews like a tutorial,
you’ll struggle in a production-focused SRE round.

